Replying to the first of the questions which y:m propound, viz, whether or not in view of the favorable reports made by officers of the Navy upon the U. S. S. Holland since she has been in the possession of the Government, I agree with those officersastothegreat value of thi\"' type of boats for harbor and coast defense, I have to state that I do, and that my opinion as to its value has not changed since my testimony before the House Committee on Naval Affairs in April last . What did Admiral Dewey say at that time?","text_hash":"f058ecfdd2b484ffa105125773c7d7e8dd50b9fa79b2bbbbe40b711fbfa1e57b"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU3-E3","locator":"DVSU3-E3; R1-E3; source=https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34/pdf/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34-2.pdf#page=77","original_excerpt_id":"R1-E3","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU3","source_tier":1,"source_title":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34/pdf/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34-2.pdf#page=77","source_url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34/pdf/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34-2.pdf#page=77","text":"Here is what Admiral Dewey says in a letter to Senator STEW­ ART: Replying to the first of the questions which y:m propound, viz, whether or not in view of the favorable reports made by officers of the Navy upon the U. S. S. Holland since she has been in the possession of the Government, I agree with those officersastothegreat value of thi\"' type of boats for harbor and coast defense, I have to state that I do, and that my opinion as to its value has not changed since my testimony before the House Committee on Naval Affairs in April last . What did Admiral Dewey say at that time? Let me read it: Gentlemen, I saw the operation of the boat down off .Mount Vernon the oth e r day.","text_hash":"4e7bf453a8aae75b341170082eb5f25c52983feb55cfbc71cf1ec23a2b629961"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E1","locator":"DVSU4-E1; R2-E1; source=https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","original_excerpt_id":"R2-E1","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU4","source_tier":2,"source_title":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","source_url":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","text":"John Philip Holland Submarine design, 1870s–1900s Irish immigrant John Philip Holland designed and constructed the U.S. Navy’s first submarine, USS Holland (SS 1). John Holland began sketching submarine designs as early as 1869 and built five experimental submarines between 1878 and 1895 before arriving at Holland ’s more successful design. Holland included all the major components of a modern submarine: dual propulsion systems, a fixed center of gravity, separate main and auxiliary ballast systems, a hydrodynamic shape, and a modern weapons system. After two years of Navy trials and modifications to Holland, the Navy purchased the submarine on April 11, 1900, for $165,000 (about $4.7 million today). It also ordered seven more Holland -type boats, sufficiently impressed by Holland ’s potential. On October 12, 1900, the U.S. Navy commissioned USS Holland as its first submarine, establishing the U.S. Submarine Force. USS Holland spent most of her ten years in service at the U.S. Naval Academy as a training submarine. The seven new Holland- type submarines, built with Navy-specified improvements, became the Navy’s A -class of submarines.","text_hash":"ad3794f3e8d11f47f5143e1359bfd3e8da5e14dc5e95ad5b8f3ed4bca80dba8c"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E2","locator":"DVSU4-E2; R2-E2; source=https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","original_excerpt_id":"R2-E2","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU4","source_tier":2,"source_title":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","source_url":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","text":"USS Holland spent most of her ten years in service at the U.S. Naval Academy as a training submarine. The seven new Holland- type submarines, built with Navy-specified improvements, became the Navy’s A -class of submarines.","text_hash":"e8753e4b42f7d41f80863fc1f13af139d5f72ebd8c3801599e98eee991b73286"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E3","locator":"DVSU4-E3; R2-E4; source=https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","original_excerpt_id":"R2-E4","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU4","source_tier":2,"source_title":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","source_url":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","text":"Navy commissioned USS Holland as its first submarine, establishing the U.S.","text_hash":"d666bd9915d6efcf4ae35a090ea91a3c39635ea5b45151ef6ce1f9faf16d7851"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E4","locator":"DVSU4-E4; R2-E5; source=https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","original_excerpt_id":"R2-E5","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU4","source_tier":2,"source_title":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","source_url":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","text":"USS Holland spent most of her ten years in service at the U.S.","text_hash":"782bd54f72cfe2789f4b2f24582d26a445eccba6e8bb41ed24c69de7c9a22cfd"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E5","locator":"DVSU4-E5; R2-E6; source=https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","original_excerpt_id":"R2-E6","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU4","source_tier":2,"source_title":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","source_url":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","text":"Navy commissioned USS Holland as its first submarine, establishing the U.S. Submarine Force.","text_hash":"b2e4c99e15d867a8e1ae80ddfbdfc1266a6bef320c4d02dce1f9ef6b263552af"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E6","locator":"DVSU4-E6; R2-E7; source=https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","original_excerpt_id":"R2-E7","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU4","source_tier":2,"source_title":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","source_url":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","text":"USS Holland spent most of her ten years in service at the U.S. Naval Academy as a training submarine.","text_hash":"56ea95b6324b5793460710b6e1d6eb485adc3f5f561539c891d042abd80519dd"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E7","locator":"DVSU4-E7; R2-E9; source=https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","original_excerpt_id":"R2-E9","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU4","source_tier":2,"source_title":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","source_url":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","text":"Navy commissioned USS Holland as its first submarine, establishing the U.S. Submarine Force. USS Holland spent most of her ten years in service at the U.S.","text_hash":"fbcc56a01db13d9bbbc13276526fcc16ffcfebec7719278c5f4496654a651a9a"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU5-E1","locator":"DVSU5-E1; R3-E2; source=https://submarinemuseum.au/explore/australia-submarine/submarines/a-history-of-submarines/","original_excerpt_id":"R3-E2","source_capture_method":"fetched_page","source_id":"DVSU5","source_tier":2,"source_title":"https://submarinemuseum.au/explore/australia-submarine/submarines/a-history-of-submarines/","source_url":"https://submarinemuseum.au/explore/australia-submarine/submarines/a-history-of-submarines/","text":"John Philip Holland Father of today’s Submarines - 1898 “John Philip Holland (born February 24?, 1841, Liscannor, County Clare, Ireland—died August 12, 1914, Newark, New Jersey, U.S.) was an Irish-born inventor who was the father of the modern submarine. He designed and built the first underwater vessel accepted by the U.S. Navy.” See page for author, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons John Philip Holland Father of today’s Submarines - 1898 “John Philip Holland (born February 24?, 1841, Liscannor, County Clare, Ireland—died August 12, 1914, Newark, New Jersey, U.S.) was an Irish-born inventor who was the father of the modern submarine. He designed and built the first underwater vessel accepted by the U.S. Navy.” Following his work in the early 1890s, the design that John Holland presented to the US Navy in 1898 has formed the basis of the designs for all submarines built since. These features include: “Efficient hydrodynamic hull shape [teardrop-shape with bulbous bow and tapered stern] with good seakeeping ability on the open ocean. Separate main and auxiliary ballast systems enable rapid diving and surfacing with minimial changes to the longitudinal center of gravity while underway. Accomplished by operating with full or nearly full ballast tanks when submerged. Allowed precise control of trim angle while submerged. Able to dive to and accurately maintain a significant depth [up of 75 feet (23 m)]. Diving planes provide the means to precisely control depth [stern planes only, located behind the propeller]. Dual propulsion systems driving a single propeller at the stern. Internal combustion engine provides reliable power on the surface, enabling long transits while charging the batteries [up to 200 nautical miles (370 km) at 6 knots] Lead-acid storage batteries provide power to run submerged for a considerable distance [about 30 nautical miles (56 km) at 5.5 knots]. Conning tower for directing ship and weapons activities on the surface or semi-submerged. No periscope. View ports around the top of the conning tower provided the commander with intermittent views while “porpoising” semi-submerged near the surface. Offensive weapons systems. One reloadable torpedo tube at the bow, with three self-propelled torpedoes carried internally. One pneumatic dynamite gun at the bow that, on the surface, fired large projectiles, sometimes called “aerial torpedoes.” [This was subsequently removed]. John P. Holland first demonstrated the Holland VI to the U.S. Navy on 17 March 1898.” 18 https://lynceans.org/all-posts/a-brief-look-back-at-the-worlds-first-modern-submarine-2/ USS Holland (SS-1) internal and external arrangements. The interior space was one contiguous compartment.","text_hash":"88650f850efb886582a258207a98742c78f7aaa4a9c4af83d60f2312136f6b56"}],"claim_assessment":{"calibration":"not_calibrated","claims":[{"claim":"Admiral Dewey attributed great value to USS Holland's type for harbor and coast defense based on favorable Navy officer reports.","counterevidence":[],"evidence":[{"excerpt_id":"DVSU3-E1","locator":"DVSU3-E1; R1-E1; source=https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34/pdf/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34-2.pdf#page=77","quote":"I agree with those officersastothegreat value of thi\"' type of boats for harbor and coast defense","source_title":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34/pdf/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34-2.pdf#page=77","source_url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34/pdf/GPO-CRECB-1901-pt4-v34-2.pdf#page=77"},{"excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E1","locator":"DVSU4-E1; R2-E1; source=https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","quote":"designed and constructed the U.S. Navy’s first submarine, USS Holland (SS 1)","source_title":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/","source_url":"https://navalunderseamuseum.org/undersea-pioneers2/"}],"id":"C1","identity_reviews":[{"anchor_excerpt_id":"DVSU4-E1","excerpt_id":"DVSU3-E1","reason":"Congressional record discusses U.S.S. Holland after Navy acquisition in 1900; DVSU4-E1 confirms USS Holland (SS 1) as the Navy's first submarine purchased April 11, 1900","status":"same_machine"}],"narrative_roles":["intended_role"],"reason":"Admiral Dewey's 1901 letter explicitly endorses the Holland type's value for harbor and coast defense. The identity_requires_review excerpt is anchored by DVSU4-E1 which confirms USS Holland (SS 1) as the Navy's first submarine purchased in 1900.","scope":"machine","status":"supported"},{"claim":"USS Holland incorporated dual propulsion systems, fixed center of gravity, separate main and auxiliary ballast systems, hydrodynamic shape, and modern weapons system.",